<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · Love song · c. 1666 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Barbara Allen</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old ballad sung for hundreds of years in Britain, Ireland and America, about a love that came too late.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>English, Scottish and Irish ballad singers; later North American, especially Appalachian, singers</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Britain</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>17th century or earlier; first mentioned 2 January 1666</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad · Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional (first mentioned 1666; earliest printed text c. 1690))</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbara-allen">Barbara Allen</a>&quot; (Child 84, Roud 54) is a traditional ballad that began in the 17th century or earlier. The earliest surviving mention is in the diary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-pepys">Samuel Pepys</a> for 2 January 1666, who at a New Year's party delighted in hearing the actress and singer Elizabeth Knepp sing &quot;her little Scotch song of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> Allen&quot;. The folklorists Steve Roud and Julia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> inferred that it was already popular and perhaps written for the stage; Phillips Barry and Fannie Hardy Eckstorm thought it was instead a libel on Barbara Villiers, mistress of Charles II, and Charles Seeger noted that Pepys would have relished such a song.</p><p>It spread quickly, both by word of mouth and in print, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Ireland and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> broadside of about 1690, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbara-allen">Barbara Allen</a>'s cruelty: or, the young-man's tragedy&quot;, is the earliest text. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">Printings</a> were common through the 18th century: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allan-ramsay-poet">Allan Ramsay</a> published &quot;Bonny Barbara Allen&quot; in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">Tea</a>-Table Miscellany (1740), and Thomas Percy two versions in his Reliques of Ancient English Poetry (1765). In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-james-child">Francis James Child</a> combined these and versions from the Roxburghe Ballads into the standard texts used by later scholars. In 1792 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-haydn">Joseph Haydn</a> arranged it, one of more than 400 folk-song arrangements commissioned by George Thomson and the publishers William Napier and William Whyte, probably taking the melody from James Oswald's Caledonian Pocket Companion of about 1750. The ballad was first printed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1836 and went on appearing on broadsides, in songbooks and in newspaper columns there.</p><p>Because it was so often printed, its words vary less than those of most folk songs: broadsides kept &quot;correcting&quot; the oral versions. Some details still change. It may be set in &quot;Scarlet Town&quot; (perhaps a pun on Reading), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, and may open at Martinmas, Lammas or in May; the Martinmas versions, most common in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, are thought to be older. The dying man is Sir John Graeme in the earliest prints and often William, James or Jimmy in America. The folklorist Harry Smith used to ask people to sing a verse and then tell them which county they were born in. The tunes, by contrast, were rarely printed and passed from singer to singer: many American tunes are pentatonic, English ones mostly major, and the minor Scottish tune in Oswald's collection seems to be the oldest.</p><p>Roud and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> called it &quot;far and away the most widely collected song in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-language">English language</a>&quot;, and the Vaughan Williams Memorial Library counts about 500 traditional recordings. The earliest is probably Percy Grainger's wax cylinder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a> singer Joseph Taylor in 1907. Others include Hule &quot;Queen&quot; Hines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> (1939), Phil Tanner (1949), Elizabeth Cronin (early 1950s), Sam Larner (1958) and Jean Ritchie (1961). Charles Seeger edited a Library of Congress record of 30 American versions recorded between 1933 and 1954.</p><p>Roger Quilter arranged it in 1921, and in the folk revival of the 1950s and 1960s it was recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a>, Eddy Arnold, the Everly Brothers, Joan Baez, Bob Dylan, and Simon and Garfunkel, among others. Dylan wrote that without &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbara-allen">Barbara Allen</a>&quot; there would be no &quot;Girl from the North Country&quot;. It has also been retold in a ghost story by Robert E. Howard, the 1942 play Dark of the Moon and radio dramas of 1952 and 2009.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A servant fetches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbara-allen">Barbara Allen</a> to the bedside of a young man dying for love of her; she turns him away, recalling a slight, and after his death she is overcome with grief and dies too. Buried in the same churchyard, they are joined at last by a rose and a briar that grow from their graves and twine into a lovers' knot. The mood is sorrowful and full of regret.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In Scarlet Town, where I was bound,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There was a fair maid dwelling,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Whom I had chosen to be my own,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And her name it was Barbara Allen.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All in the merry month of May,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When green leaves they was springing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>This young man on his death-bed lay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For the love of Barbara Allen.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He sent his man unto her then,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To the town where she was dwelling:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'You must come to my master dear,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>If your name be Barbara Allen.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'For death is printed in his face,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And sorrow's in him dwelling,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And you must come to my master dear,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>If your name be Barbara Allen.'</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 60 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'If death be printed in his face,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And sorrow's in him dwelling,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then little better shall he be</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For bonny Barbara Allen.'</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So slowly, slowly she got up,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And so slowly she came to him,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And all she said when she came there,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Young man, I think you are a dying.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He turnd his face unto her then:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'If you be Barbara Allen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>My dear,' said he, 'Come pitty me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As on my death-bed I am lying.'</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'If on your death-bed you be lying,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>What is that to Barbara Allen?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I cannot keep you from [your] death;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So farewell,' said Barbara Allen.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He turnd his face unto the wall,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And death came creeping to him:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Then adieu, adieu, and adieu to all,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And adieu to Barbara Allen!'</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And as she was walking on a day,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>She heard the bell a ringing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And it did seem to ring to her</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Unworthy Barbara Allen.'</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>She turnd herself round about,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And she spy'd the corps a coming:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Lay down, lay down the corps of clay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That I may look upon him.'</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And all the while she looked on,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So loudly she lay laughing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>While all her friends cry'd [out] amain,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Unworthy Barbara Allen!'</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When he was dead, and laid in grave,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then death came creeping to she:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'O mother, mother, make my bed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For his death hath quite undone me.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'A hard-hearted creature that I was,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To slight one that lovd me so dearly;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I wish I had been more kinder to him,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The time of his life when he was near me.'</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So this maid she then did dye,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And desired to be buried by him,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And repented her self before she dy'd,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That ever she did deny him.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional; the broadside &quot;Barbara Allen's Cruelty&quot; (late 17th century), as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbara_Allen_(song)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
